 "build": {
  "id": "skins",
  "title": "Skins: one look per app, skin@1 + app-look@1, the Windows skin, Settings > Appearance as the Theme manager",
  "profile": "all",
  "category": "Design",
  "priority": "",
  "repo": "amberx-axf"
 },
 "said": {
  "text": "Can the design pack alone make the app look exactly like a Windows program? ... We'll call it a skin. It will include its own assets, its own layout, its own colors, and its own style. You use the style theme, a layout theme and a color theme, and call it a skin. ... I don't want all the apps to inherit just file and colors from that, so I want there to be a switch to do it, but by default they're all their own. And every one has a reset, restore to default. So as of now, store the apps as default, for all. And then create a new one. ... Okay, Opus, but add it for a possible later review.",
  "verbatim": true,
  "at": "2026-11-04T00:00:00Z",
  "source": "voice",
  "by": "",
  "addenda": []
 },
 "brief": "# Skins upgrade — cover all new asset types (Brett)\nUpgrade the skins system so it styles EVERY asset type now in the registry, not just the original kinds.\n- Cover the new types: embeddable widget, icon pack, composite assets (button = box + text, nav = container + items), data set bar (search/filter/sort), embed, and every element down to text blocks/labels.\n- Skins apply at any scope: global/app/UI/plugin, with child-overrides-parent inheritance (same model as design/icon packs).\n- A skin is a design pack; it MAY reference an icon pack but doesn't have to.\n- Pull the full asset-type list from the registry dynamically so new types are covered automatically going forward (no hardcoded list); add a CI check that fails if a registered asset type has no skin coverage.\n- Voice + API/MCP/CLI parity. Submit at least 3 UI options to UI Review first.",
 "models": [
  "Opus",
  "Fable"
 ],
 "accounts": [
  "account-1"
 ],
